On Thu, May 2, 2013 at 6:56 PM, Jorge Llamb=EDas <jjllambias@gmail.com> wro=
te:

> I would say that if you are using "lo [ca] me mi ku" instead of plain "mi=
"
> then chances are you must be thinking of a plural "mi", be it spatially o=
r
> temporally. Otherwise why wouldn't you use plain "mi" instead? If you use
> "ca", then the suggestion is that the plurality occurs temporally
> (otherwise you would use something like "lo vi me mi", "lo vu me mi" to
> restrict it. This of course is not a full specification. As usual, the
> price of infinite precision is infinite verbosity. You could say "lo ca
> jenai pu me mi" to select the present me that excludes the past one, You
> could use "lo ze'e me mi" to indicate the one that has always been and
> always will be me and so on. I don't expect these kind of expressions to
> become common though because it doesn't seem to make much difference
> whether we think of objects as (time non-local) wholes or as strings of
> (time local) stages. For a distinction to be strongly marked in language =
it
> has to make a relevant difference.
>
This amounts to saying it can be local or nonlocal, but doesn't specify how
to *explicitly* state the distinction. The ability to make such
specifications (if the language supports them at all) is pretty much my
third axiom for Lojban design :)

mi'e la latro'a mu'o

